\description{
stats.ncaa.org gives a team a new numeric id every season
and spells school names in AP style; ESPN keeps one id per school. These
loaders read the all-seasons crosswalk tables on the \code{mbb_crosswalk}
release tag that join the two, so the \verb{load_ncaa_mbb_*()} tables can be
matched to the ESPN-based \verb{load_mbb_*()} tables.
\itemize{
\item \code{load_mbb_ncaa_espn_team_crosswalk()}: one row per season and
stats.ncaa.org team id (2009-10 onward), with the ESPN team and both
sources' conference for that season. The same table as
sportsdataverse-py's \code{ncaa_espn_team_crosswalk(league = "mbb")}.
\item \code{load_mbb_ncaa_espn_schools()}: one row per NCAA school (org id)
with its ESPN team.
\item \code{load_mbb_ncaa_espn_school_seasons()}: one row per school, sport and
season with that season's stats.ncaa.org team id. It covers both
sports (\code{sport} is \code{mbb} or \code{wbb}), since an org id is shared by every
team of a school.
}

The ESPN ids (\code{espn_team_id}, \code{espn_conference_id}) are returned as
integer, matching the ESPN loaders. \code{ncaa_team_id} and \code{ncaa_org_id}
are integer, as published and as in sportsdataverse-py and wehoop; the
\verb{load_ncaa_mbb_*()} loaders keep the NCAA ids as character (e.g. the
\code{id} of \code{\link[=load_ncaa_mbb_team_ids]{load_ncaa_mbb_team_ids()}}), so cast one side before joining
them. \code{season} is the
\code{"YYYY-YY"} string the release uses. The files are parquet, so these
loaders need the arrow package.
}
